{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":151270,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"da26dcd9-7afc-51dd-8062-49f76ecfee30","total_cost_usd":0.391661,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6409,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":12969,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6409,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":12969,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6409,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6409},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3256,"outputTokens":16,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0033360000000000004,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":12969,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":6409,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.388325,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\": \"actor:dependence-a-reviewer-opus-08\", \"reviews\": [{\"case_id\": \"case:aff3487b2c9451dcaddd\", \"verdict\": \"demonstrated_issue\", \"bounded_statement\": \"The selected result in results/report.md was produced by feeding both measurements of every source item into a two-independent-samples t-test: the 12 distinct `k1`,`k2` source items each contribute one value to `left` (column `a`) and one value to `right` (column `b`), so each authorized independent unit enters the row-independent procedure twice and the reported df=22.0 asserts 24 mutually independent observations where only 12 independent units exist.\", \"root_cause\": \"workflow/analysis.py constructs the two comparison arms as two columns of the same staged row set (`left` from `a`, `right` from `b`) and passes them to `scipy.stats.ttest_ind`, a procedure whose null distribution assumes every entry in both arms comes from a distinct independent unit. Because task.md designates each ordered `k1`,`k2` pair (i.e. each row here) as one source item, `a` and `b` on a row are two measurements of the same unit, and the within-unit pairing is discarded rather than modelled (no paired/within-unit test, no aggregation to one entry per unit).\", \"issue_class\": \"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\", \"evidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"task.md defines the ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as the source item, so both numeric columns on a given row belong to the same source item.\", \"observed_value\": \"unit key = ordered (k1,k2) pair\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item; rows carrying the same ordered pair originate from the same source item.\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The workflow builds the two arms from columns `a` and `b` of the identical row list, so every staged row contributes exactly one entry to each arm.\", \"observed_value\": \"left and right iterate over the same `staged` sequence\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 6, \"end_line\": 8, \"quoted_text\": \"staged = rows\\nleft = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in staged]\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The procedure applied is the independent-two-sample t-test, which treats all entries across both arms as independent observations.\", \"observed_value\": \"scipy.stats.ttest_ind\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 3, \"end_line\": 3, \"quoted_text\": \"from scipy.stats import ttest_ind\"}, {\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 9, \"end_line\": 9, \"quoted_text\": \"result = ttest_ind(left, right)\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The staged data contains 12 rows, i.e. 12 source items supplying 24 numeric measurements in total.\", \"observed_value\": 12, \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,shared,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,shared,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,shared,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,shared,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,shared,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,shared,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,shared,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,shared,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,shared,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,shared,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,shared,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,shared,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The selected report's degrees of freedom quantify the inflation: df=22.0 corresponds to n1+n2-2 = 24-2, i.e. 24 assumed-independent observations, versus 12 authorized independent units (a within-unit analysis of the same rows would carry 11 df).\", \"observed_value\": \"df=22.0 (24 assumed-independent entries from 12 units)\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"[selected-result] TtestResult(statistic=np.float64(-0.6793662204867575), pvalue=np.float64(0.5039915691282064), df=np.float64(22.0))\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"No source item supplies more than one row: all 12 ordered `k1`,`k2` pairs are distinct, so the narrower reading of the issue class (a unit duplicated across rows) is not instantiated; the repeated entry occurs across columns within a row rather than across rows.\", \"observed_value\": \"12 distinct (k1,k2) pairs in 12 rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,shared,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,shared,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,shared,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,shared,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,shared,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,shared,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,shared,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,shared,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,shared,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,shared,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,shared,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,shared,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The `tag` column holds the constant value `shared` on every row, which could be read as an even coarser grouping (one item for all rows); task.md, however, fixes the unit key as the ordered `k1`,`k2` pair, so `tag` does not define the unit and is not the basis of this verdict.\", \"observed_value\": \"tag == \\\"shared\\\" on all 12 rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 2, \"quoted_text\": \"k1,k2,tag,a,b\\nu01,v01,shared,1.0,2.0\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"I first tried to falsify the verdict by looking for the most literal instantiation of the class and finding it absent: I checked every row key and confirmed all 12 ordered `k1`,`k2` pairs are unique, so no unit is repeated across rows, and I checked whether the workflow subsets, deduplicates or aggregates rows before testing (it does not; `staged = rows`). Under a reading of the class restricted to duplicated row keys, the verdict would flip to negative. I then tested whether the surviving reading could be explained away: could `a` and `b` legitimately come from different units? task.md states the ordered pair identifies the source item and each row carries exactly one such pair with both `a` and `b`, so the two arms are two measurements of the same 12 units, not 24 units. Could the report nonetheless reflect a unit-respecting analysis? No: df=22.0 is exactly n1+n2-2 for 12+12 entries, which is only correct if all 24 entries are independent, and `ttest_ind` offers no within-unit adjustment. I also considered the constant `tag` column as an alternative dependence story and rejected it as a basis, since the task fixes the unit key elsewhere; the verdict does not depend on `tag`. The remaining uncertainty is purely about how broadly the class name is scoped, not about the observed facts.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"medium\"}, {\"case_id\": \"case:ede79e7dd2b73963ae43\", \"verdict\": \"demonstrated_issue\", \"bounded_statement\": \"The selected result in results/report.md comes from an independent-two-sample t-test in which each of the 12 distinct `k1`,`k2` source items contributes one entry to `left` (column `a`) and one entry to `right` (column `b`); the same authorized independent unit therefore enters the row-independent procedure twice, and the reported df=22.0 asserts 24 independent observations drawn from only 12 units.\", \"root_cause\": \"workflow/analysis.py forms both comparison arms from two columns of the identical staged row list and passes them to `scipy.stats.ttest_ind`, whose independence assumption requires every entry in both arms to come from a distinct unit. Since task.md designates each ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item, `a` and `b` on a row are repeated measurements of one unit; the workflow neither pairs them nor reduces each unit to a single entry.\", \"issue_class\": \"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\", \"evidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"task.md fixes the authorized independent unit as the ordered `k1`,`k2` pair, so the two numeric fields on a row are measurements of the same unit.\", \"observed_value\": \"unit key = ordered (k1,k2) pair\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item; rows carrying the same ordered pair originate from the same source item.\"}]}, {\"description\": \"Both arms are built by iterating the same staged row sequence, one arm per column, so each row (unit) supplies exactly one entry to each arm.\", \"observed_value\": \"left from column a, right from column b, same `staged` list\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 6, \"end_line\": 8, \"quoted_text\": \"staged = rows\\nleft = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in staged]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in staged]\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The named procedure is the independent-samples t-test, which treats every entry in the two arms as an independent observation.\", \"observed_value\": \"scipy.stats.ttest_ind\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 9, \"end_line\": 9, \"quoted_text\": \"result = ttest_ind(left, right)\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The staged file supplies 12 rows / 12 source items and therefore 24 measurements in total.\", \"observed_value\": 12, \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The reported degrees of freedom make the double entry explicit and quantify the resulting inflation of the effective sample size.\", \"observed_value\": \"df=22.0 = 24 - 2, versus 11 df for 12 independent units analysed within-unit\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"[selected-result] TtestResult(statistic=np.float64(-0.6793662204867575), pvalue=np.float64(0.5039915691282064), df=np.float64(22.0))\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"Every ordered `k1`,`k2` pair occurs exactly once, so no unit is duplicated across rows; the repeated entry arises across the two measurement columns of a single row, not from row-level replication.\", \"observed_value\": \"12 distinct (k1,k2) pairs in 12 rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The `tag` column is unique per row (t01..t12) and thus offers no competing clustering structure that could be blamed for the dependence; the dependence is entirely the within-row pairing of `a` and `b`.\", \"observed_value\": \"tag values t01..t12, one per row\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 3, \"quoted_text\": \"k1,k2,tag,a,b\\nu01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"To falsify, I searched the staged file for any repeated ordered `k1`,`k2` pair, since the most literal form of this class is one unit appearing in several rows; none exists, and the workflow performs no filtering, deduplication or aggregation (`staged = rows`), so under a strictly row-duplication reading the finding would not stand. I then tried to explain away the surviving mechanism: if `a` and `b` originated from different source items, the two arms would be independent, but task.md states the ordered pair identifies the source item and each row carries a single pair together with both `a` and `b`, so the arms are matched measurements on the same 12 units. I also checked whether the report could still be a unit-respecting analysis despite the code: df=22.0 equals n1+n2-2 for 12+12 entries and could only arise from treating all 24 measurements as independent, and `ttest_ind` provides no within-unit correction. No alternative reading of the visible files reconciles 24 independent observations with 12 authorized units, so the verdict survives every falsification route available in these files.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"high\"}, {\"case_id\": \"case:f170479fec4de0e31a85\", \"verdict\": \"demonstrated_issue\", \"bounded_statement\": \"The selected result in results/report.md is a Mann-Whitney U test computed on two arms taken from columns `a` and `b` of the same 12 rows, so each of the 12 distinct `k1`,`k2` source items enters this two-independent-samples rank procedure twice; the reported statistic 60.5 lies on the U scale for n1=n2=12, i.e. 24 observations treated as mutually independent when only 12 authorized independent units exist.\", \"root_cause\": \"workflow/analysis.py derives `left` and `right` from two columns of the identical row list and passes them to `scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu`, whose null distribution is the distribution of ranks under mutual independence of all entries in both arms. Because task.md designates each ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item, `a` and `b` on a row are two measurements of the same unit; the workflow discards that within-unit matching instead of using a within-unit rank procedure or reducing each unit to one entry.\", \"issue_class\": \"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\", \"evidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"task.md designates the ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as the source item, making `a` and `b` on a row two measurements of one unit.\", \"observed_value\": \"unit key = ordered (k1,k2) pair\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"task.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"Treat each distinct ordered `k1`,`k2` pair as one source item; rows carrying the same ordered pair originate from the same source item.\"}]}, {\"description\": \"Both comparison arms are comprehensions over the same `rows` list, one per column, so each unit contributes exactly one entry to each arm.\", \"observed_value\": \"left and right both iterate `rows`\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 6, \"end_line\": 7, \"quoted_text\": \"left = [float(row[\\\"a\\\"]) for row in rows]\\nright = [float(row[\\\"b\\\"]) for row in rows]\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The procedure applied is the two-independent-samples Mann-Whitney U test, which assumes independence both within and between the two arms.\", \"observed_value\": \"scipy.stats.mannwhitneyu\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 3, \"end_line\": 3, \"quoted_text\": \"from scipy.stats import mannwhitneyu\"}, {\"path\": \"workflow/analysis.py\", \"start_line\": 8, \"end_line\": 8, \"quoted_text\": \"result = mannwhitneyu(left, right)\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The staged file supplies 12 rows / 12 source items, each carrying both an `a` and a `b` measurement, for 24 measurements fed to the test.\", \"observed_value\": 12, \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"The selected result is reported on the between-groups U scale (maximum 12*12=144, midpoint 72), confirming the arms were compared as two independent groups of 12 rather than as 12 matched units.\", \"observed_value\": \"statistic=60.5 on the U scale for n1=n2=12\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"[selected-result] MannwhitneyuResult(statistic=np.float64(60.5), pvalue=np.float64(0.5243792697676437))\"}]}], \"counterevidence_atoms\": [{\"description\": \"No ordered `k1`,`k2` pair repeats across rows, so the issue is not instantiated as row-level duplication of a unit; it arises from the same unit supplying one entry to each arm.\", \"observed_value\": \"12 distinct (k1,k2) pairs in 12 rows\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/data.csv\", \"start_line\": 2, \"end_line\": 13, \"quoted_text\": \"u01,v01,t01,1.0,2.0\\nu02,v02,t02,2.0,4.0\\nu03,v03,t03,3.0,3.0\\nu04,v04,t04,4.0,5.0\\nu05,v05,t05,5.0,7.0\\nu06,v06,t06,6.0,6.0\\nu07,v07,t07,7.0,9.0\\nu08,v08,t08,8.0,8.0\\nu09,v09,t09,9.0,11.0\\nu10,v10,t10,10.0,10.0\\nu11,v11,t11,11.0,13.0\\nu12,v12,t12,12.0,12.0\"}]}, {\"description\": \"Unlike a t-test result, the reported output contains no degrees-of-freedom field, so the effective sample size is inferred from the U statistic's scale rather than stated outright in the report.\", \"observed_value\": \"report contains only statistic and pvalue\", \"source_spans\": [{\"path\": \"results/report.md\", \"start_line\": 1, \"end_line\": 1, \"quoted_text\": \"MannwhitneyuResult(statistic=np.float64(60.5), pvalue=np.float64(0.5243792697676437))\"}]}], \"falsification_attempt\": \"I attempted three refutations. (1) Row-level duplication: I checked all 12 ordered `k1`,`k2` pairs and found them distinct, and the workflow applies no grouping or aggregation before testing, so under a reading of the class limited to a unit spanning multiple rows the finding would fail; the mechanism here is instead one unit supplying one entry to each arm. (2) Independent arms: if `a` and `b` came from different source items the test would be appropriate, but task.md ties the source item to the ordered pair carried by the row that holds both values, so the arms are matched on the same 12 units. (3) Unit-respecting output: I checked whether the reported number could have come from a within-unit rank procedure; a signed-rank statistic on 12 pairs is bounded by 78 and `mannwhitneyu` computes only the between-groups U (bounded by 144, here 60.5 with a half-integer from ties across arms), and the call site passes the two raw column lists, so the reported value can only be the independent-groups statistic over 24 entries. The verdict survives; the residual uncertainty is the absence of an explicit df field, which the U scale compensates for.\", \"cross_case_evidence_used\": false, \"unresolved_material_questions\": [], \"self_reported_confidence\": \"high\"}]}","ttft_ms":73216,"ttft_stream_ms":2126,"time_to_request_ms":252,"type":"result","duration_ms":152333,"uuid":"203ba476-4835-4344-85c4-701d866e715e"}
